a_n = a_{n-1} + 2a_{n-2} + 2a_{n-3} + 2a_{n-4} + 2a_{n-5} + 2a_{n-6} + 2a_{n-7}
\] for \(n \geq 7\), with the initial conditions: \(a_0 = 1\), \(a_1 = 3\), \(a_2 = 9\), \(a_3 = 27\), \(a_4 = 81\), \(a_5 = 243\), \(a_6 = 729\). We need to compute \(a_7\) and ensure that all three colors are used at least once. The number of ways to tile a \(7 \times 1\) board using only red, blue, and green tiles without any color restrictions is \(3^7 = 2187\). The number of ways to tile a \(7 \times 1\) board using only two colors is \(3 \cdot 2^7 - 3 = 3 \cdot 128 - 3 = 381\) (we subtract 3 to exclude the cases where only one color is used). Therefore, the number of ways to tile a \(7 \times 1\) board using all three colors is \(2187 - 381 = 1806\). The final answer is the remainder when 1806 is divided by 1000, which is 806. </think> <answer>806</answer>
